from full_a import C
LONG = {}

LONG["joshua"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Joshua was", """Joshua, the son of Nun, was born a slave in Egypt, from the tribe of Ephraim. His original name was Hoshea, “salvation.” Moses changed it to Joshua, “the Lord saves.” In Greek, the same name is Jesus. He was Moses’ assistant for about forty years, and then he led Israel into the promised land around 1400 BC.

He was a soldier, a leader and, above all, a man who trusted God to do what He said He would do.""", ("Joshua", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Joshua’s story is about taking God at His word, finishing what someone else started, and the battles that come before a promise is fully received. It also gives some of the Bible’s best-known calls to courage.""")],
cards=[
C("Moses’ assistant", "Joshua led the first battle against Amalek, served in the tent of meeting and was one of the twelve spies.",
"""Joshua first appears just after the Israelites left Egypt. A fierce desert tribe, the Amalekites, attacked them. Moses told Joshua to choose men and fight. While Joshua fought in the valley, Moses stood on a hilltop with the staff of God in his hands. When Moses’ arms were up, Israel prevailed; when they dropped, Amalek did. Aaron and Hur held up Moses’ arms until sunset, and “Joshua overwhelmed Amalek and his people with the sword.”

Joshua was also Moses’ close helper. When Moses went up Mount Sinai, Joshua went partway with him. When Moses met with God in the tent outside the camp, Joshua stayed in the tent: “When Moses turned again into the camp, his assistant Joshua the son of Nun, a young man, would not depart from the tent.” He also went as one of the twelve spies and, along with Caleb, brought back a faithful report. For that, God said they would be the only two of their generation to enter the promised land.""", ("Exodus", 17)),
C("“Be strong and courageous”", "After Moses died, God told Joshua to lead the people into the land and to keep the Book of the Law close.",
"""Moses died on Mount Nebo, in sight of the land. Joshua, perhaps about eighty, was now the leader of two million people and an unfinished task. God spoke to him: “Moses my servant is dead. Now therefore arise, go over this Jordan, you and all this people, into the land that I am giving to them.”

Three times in one chapter, God told him to be strong and courageous. “No man shall be able to stand before you all the days of your life. Just as I was with Moses, so I will be with you. I will not leave you or forsake you.” He also told him how: “This Book of the Law shall not depart from your mouth, but you shall meditate on it day and night, so that you may be careful to do according to all that is written in it. For then you will make your way prosperous, and then you will have good success.” Courage comes from knowing what God has said.

“Have I not commanded you? Be strong and courageous. Do not be frightened, and do not be dismayed, for the Lord your God is with you wherever you go.”""", ("Joshua", 1)),
C("Crossing the Jordan", "The priests carried the ark into the flooded Jordan River, and the water stopped flowing so Israel crossed on dry ground.",
"""It was the time of the spring harvest, and the Jordan River was at flood stage. Joshua told the priests to carry the ark of the covenant to the water’s edge. “As soon as the soles of the feet of the priests bearing the ark of the Lord... rest in the waters of the Jordan, the waters of the Jordan shall be cut off.” As soon as the priests’ feet touched the water, the river stopped and piled up far upstream near a town called Adam. The priests stood in the middle of the dry riverbed while the entire nation crossed.

Joshua had twelve men, one from each tribe, take a large stone from the riverbed and set them up at Gilgal, as a memorial. “When your children ask in time to come, ‘What do those stones mean to you?’ then you shall tell them that the waters of the Jordan were cut off.” After that, the manna stopped. Israel ate the produce of the land for the first time.""", ("Joshua", 3)),
C("Jericho and a setback", "Jericho fell after Israel marched around it for seven days. But Israel then lost at Ai because of Achan’s sin.",
"""Near Jericho, Joshua met a man with a drawn sword. “Are you for us, or for our adversaries?” “No; but I am the commander of the army of the Lord.” Joshua fell on his face. “Take off your sandals from your feet, for the place where you are standing is holy.” God gave Joshua a strange plan: march around the city once a day for six days, seven times on the seventh, with priests blowing trumpets. At the shout, the walls fell flat, and Israel took the city. Rahab and her family were spared.

God had commanded that everything in Jericho be devoted to Him. A man named Achan secretly kept a beautiful robe, silver and gold. When Israel attacked the little town of Ai, they were beaten, and thirty-six men died. Joshua tore his clothes and fell before the ark. God told him that someone had sinned. By lot, Achan was identified. He confessed, and he and his family were judged in the valley of Achor. After that, Israel took Ai.""", ("Joshua", 6)),
C("The long day", "At Gibeon, five kings attacked, and Joshua prayed for the sun to stand still.",
"""The people of Gibeon had tricked Joshua into a treaty, pretending to come from a far country. When five Amorite kings attacked Gibeon, Joshua marched all night from Gilgal to rescue them. The Lord threw the enemy into a panic and sent great hailstones from heaven. More died from hail than from swords.

Then Joshua spoke to the Lord in front of the people: “Sun, stand still at Gibeon, and moon, in the Valley of Aijalon.” “And the sun stood still, and the moon stopped, until the nation took vengeance on their enemies... There has been no day like it before or since, when the Lord heeded the voice of a man, for the Lord fought for Israel.” The Bible does not explain how it happened. The point is that God answered a prayer of faith in a big way. Joshua went on to conquer the south and the north over several years.""", ("Joshua", 10)),
C("“As for me and my house”", "In his old age Joshua gathered Israel at Shechem and told them to choose whom they would serve.",
"""After many years of war, the land was divided among the tribes. Joshua gave Caleb the hill country of Hebron, and took for himself a city in the hill country of Ephraim, Timnath-serah. Near the end of his life, he called all Israel to Shechem and reminded them of their history, from Abraham to the conquest. Then came a challenge: “Now therefore fear the Lord and serve him in sincerity and in faithfulness. Put away the gods that your fathers served... And if it is evil in your eyes to serve the Lord, choose this day whom you will serve... But as for me and my house, we will serve the Lord.”

The people answered, “Far be it from us that we should forsake the Lord.” Joshua warned them, “You are not able to serve the Lord.” They insisted, so he made a covenant with them and set up a large stone under an oak as a witness.

Joshua died at 110. The Bible calls him “the servant of the Lord.” He was buried at Timnath-serah. The bones of Joseph, which Israel had carried out of Egypt, were buried at Shechem. “Israel served the Lord all the days of Joshua.”""", ("Joshua", 24))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Joshua’s courage was not self-confidence. It was trust in God’s promise. God said, “I will be with you,” and that was enough. Whatever Jordan River is in front of you, this is the promise that matters.

Joshua’s story also shows that obedience is not always logical. Marching around a city, walking into a flooded river and asking the sun to stop all seem odd. But God does not always use the method we would pick. He asks us to trust.

And his famous line is a good one to keep: “Choose this day whom you will serve... as for me and my house, we will serve the Lord.” A good life and a good family are built by a decision made again and again. The name Joshua is the same as Jesus. The first Joshua led Israel into the promised land, and Jesus leads His people into God’s rest.""", ("Joshua", 24))])

LONG["jude-thaddaeus"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Jude was", """Jude, also called Thaddaeus, was one of the twelve apostles. He is listed as “Judas the son of James” in Luke and Acts, and as “Thaddaeus” in Matthew and Mark. The Bible does not say why he has two names, but it was common for people at that time to have more than one name. He is not Judas Iscariot, who betrayed Jesus. The Bible takes care to say so when it records his one known question.

He is always listed among the last four of the twelve. Though the Bible records very little about him, the one place where he speaks shows a mind that was thinking deeply about Jesus.""", ("Luke", 6)),
("Why his story matters", """Jude reminds us that every one of the twelve was a real man with real questions. His question at the Last Supper led to one of Jesus’ most important teachings about how God lives with His people.""")],
cards=[
C("One of the twelve", "Jude was called by Jesus along with the others after a night of prayer.",
"""Jesus’ disciples included Galileans and one or two men from Judea. They came from many walks of life. Jude was one of those whom Jesus chose after spending a night in prayer. Over the next three years he walked with Jesus, heard His teaching, and saw His miracles: the sick healed, the storm calmed, the crowd of thousands fed, and the dead raised.

He was also sent out with the others, two by two, to preach and heal. The Bible does not say who his partner was. But the promise Jesus gave them was clear: “Whoever receives you receives me, and whoever receives me receives him who sent me.” Jude went on a mission with the authority of Jesus.""", ("Matthew", 10)),
C("The last supper", "On the night before He died, Jesus told His disciples He would not leave them as orphans. He promised to send the Holy Spirit.",
"""On the night before the crucifixion, Jesus ate the Passover meal with the twelve, washed their feet and gave them a long talk. It was a heavy evening. Jesus said He would soon leave, that one of them would betray Him and that Peter would deny Him. The disciples were confused and afraid.

He told them, “Let not your hearts be troubled. Believe in God; believe also in me. In my Father’s house are many rooms... I go to prepare a place for you.” He promised: “I will not leave you as orphans; I will come to you.” He said He would send the Helper, the Holy Spirit, who would teach them everything. “Yet a little while and the world will see me no more, but you will see me.” The disciples did not fully understand.""", ("John", 14)),
C("Jude’s question", "Jude asked, “Lord, how is it that you will manifest yourself to us, and not to the world?”",
"""In the middle of this teaching, “Judas (not Iscariot) said to him, ‘Lord, how is it that you will manifest yourself to us, and not to the world?’” John takes care to say “not Iscariot,” to make it clear who was speaking.

Jude had picked up on something important. Jesus had said that the world would not see Him, but the disciples would. He wondered why. If Jesus was the Messiah, why not show Himself openly to everyone? Many Jews of the time expected the Messiah to come in power and be seen by all.

Jesus’ answer is one of the most beautiful in the Gospels: “If anyone loves me, he will keep my word, and my Father will love him, and we will come to him and make our home with him.” God would not just visit His people. He would live in them. Jude’s question gave Jesus a chance to explain that the Father and the Son would make their home in everyone who loves and obeys Him. “Whoever does not love me does not keep my words.”""", ("John", 14)),
C("From the upper room to the world", "After the resurrection, Jude was among the apostles in the upper room, praying until the Holy Spirit came.",
"""When Jesus was arrested, Jude fled with the others. But after the resurrection, Jesus appeared to His disciples and restored them. After the ascension, they returned to Jerusalem and went upstairs to the room where they were staying. Acts lists them by name, and one of them is “Judas the son of James.” They prayed together until Pentecost.

The promise Jude had asked about came true. The Holy Spirit came on them all, and the apostles began to preach. Jesus was now present in His people, as He had said. The Bible does not say where Jude went to preach. Traditions say he went to Mesopotamia, Syria, Persia and Armenia, and was martyred. The Bible does not confirm them, but they match how the apostles spread out across the empire.""", ("Acts", 1)),
C("The letter of Jude", "A short letter in the New Testament is written by “Jude, a servant of Jesus Christ and brother of James.”",
"""One of the shortest books of the New Testament is the letter of Jude. Its author calls himself “Jude, a servant of Jesus Christ and brother of James.” Many scholars think this was Jude, the half-brother of Jesus, who along with James is named in the Gospels as one of Jesus’ brothers. If so, he was not the apostle. The Bible does not say clearly.

The letter was written to believers who were being threatened by false teachers. “Beloved, although I was very eager to write to you about our common salvation, I found it necessary to write appealing to you to contend for the faith that was once for all delivered to the saints.” It ends with one of the best-known blessings in the Bible: “Now to him who is able to keep you from stumbling and to present you blameless before the presence of his glory with great joy, to the only God, our Savior, through Jesus Christ our Lord, be glory, majesty, dominion, and authority, before all time and now and forever. Amen.”""", ("Jude", 1))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Jude’s question teaches us that it is good to ask honest questions of Jesus. He did not pretend to understand when he did not. He asked, and the answer has encouraged believers for 2,000 years.

The answer itself is a promise: if we love Jesus and keep His word, the Father and the Son will make their home with us. The God of the universe does not only visit. He moves in.

The closing words of the letter of Jude are a prayer for the end of a hard day, or a hard life: that the One who is able to keep us from stumbling will present us blameless with great joy. We are not holding on to God alone. He is holding on to us.""", ("John", 14))])

LONG["nicodemus"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Nicodemus was", """Nicodemus was a Pharisee and a member of the Sanhedrin, the Jewish ruling council of seventy-one men. He was one of the most respected religious men in Jerusalem. Jesus calls him “the teacher of Israel.” He lived about AD 30.

The Pharisees were known for their devotion to the law of Moses and its traditions. Most of the Pharisees opposed Jesus, because He challenged their authority. But Nicodemus was different. He was curious and honest, and he kept returning.""", ("John", 3)),
("Why his story matters", """Nicodemus appears three times in the Gospel of John, and each time we see a little more of his journey. His story is about someone moving slowly from curiosity to faith, and how Jesus meets people where they are.""")],
cards=[
C("A visit at night", "Nicodemus came to Jesus at night and said, “We know that you are a teacher from God.”",
"""Early in His ministry, Jesus came to Jerusalem for the Passover and cleared the merchants out of the Temple. He also did signs that amazed people. One night, Nicodemus came to see Him. Perhaps he came at night so that no one would see him, or so they could talk without crowds. John’s Gospel often uses night and day to mean spiritual darkness and light.

He started politely: “Rabbi, we know that you are a teacher come from God, for no one can do these signs that you do unless God is with him.” Notice the “we.” Other leaders had noticed too. But Jesus skipped the small talk and went straight to the heart: “Truly, truly, I say to you, unless one is born again he cannot see the kingdom of God.”""", ("John", 3)),
C("Born again", "Jesus told Nicodemus that a person must be born of water and the Spirit. Nicodemus asked, “How can these things be?”",
"""The Greek word for “again” can also mean “from above.” Nicodemus took it literally: “How can a man be born when he is old? Can he enter a second time into his mother’s womb and be born?” Jesus explained: “Truly, truly, I say to you, unless one is born of water and the Spirit, he cannot enter the kingdom of God. That which is born of the flesh is flesh, and that which is born of the Spirit is spirit.”

He used the wind as a picture: “The wind blows where it wishes, and you hear its sound, but you do not know where it comes from or where it goes. So it is with everyone who is born of the Spirit.” You cannot control it or explain it fully, but you can see what it does. Nicodemus, the great teacher, was confused. “How can these things be?” Jesus said, “Are you the teacher of Israel and yet you do not understand these things?”""", ("John", 3)),
C("The snake and the most famous verse", "Jesus said the Son of Man must be lifted up, and “God so loved the world that he gave his only Son.”",
"""Jesus reminded Nicodemus of a story from the wilderness. When the Israelites were bitten by poisonous snakes, Moses lifted up a bronze snake on a pole, and anyone who looked at it lived. “And as Moses lifted up the serpent in the wilderness, so must the Son of Man be lifted up, that whoever believes in him may have eternal life.” It was a hint of the cross.

Then comes what is probably the best-known verse in the Bible: “For God so loved the world, that he gave his only Son, that whoever believes in him should not perish but have eternal life. For God did not send his Son into the world to condemn the world, but in order that the world might be saved through him.” The Bible does not tell us how Nicodemus responded that night. The conversation just ends. But it was a seed.""", ("John", 3)),
C("Speaking up", "Later, when other leaders wanted to arrest Jesus, Nicodemus asked, “Does our law judge a man without first hearing him?”",
"""About two years later, at the Feast of Booths, many people were saying Jesus was the Prophet or the Christ. The Pharisees sent officers to arrest Him, but they returned empty-handed. “No one ever spoke like this man!” The Pharisees were angry: “Have any of the authorities or of the Pharisees believed in him? But this crowd that does not know the law is accursed.”

Nicodemus spoke up, the first time he did so in public. “Does our law judge a man without first giving him a hearing and learning what he does?” It was a cautious statement, based on the law, and not a confession of faith. The others mocked him: “Are you from Galilee too? Search and see that no prophet arises from Galilee.” Nicodemus said no more, but he had taken a stand.""", ("John", 7)),
C("At the cross", "After Jesus died, Nicodemus and Joseph of Arimathea gave Him a proper burial.",
"""Jesus was crucified on the day before the Sabbath of Passover week. By evening, His body had to be taken down. His disciples were gone, hiding. Then two members of the Sanhedrin came forward. One was Joseph of Arimathea, a secret disciple, who asked Pilate for the body. The other was Nicodemus.

“Nicodemus, who earlier had come to Jesus by night, also came, bringing a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about seventy-five pounds in weight.” It was an enormous amount, the kind of burial spices fit for a king. Together they took Jesus’ body, wrapped it in linen cloths with the spices, “as is the burial custom of the Jews,” and laid it in a new tomb in a garden, where no one had been laid.

It was a costly act. These two men risked their positions, reputations and safety at the very moment when the disciples were hiding. Nicodemus had come to Jesus at night and now at last stood in the light.""", ("John", 19))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Nicodemus shows that faith often grows slowly. He came with questions at night, spoke up carefully, and finally acted at the cross. Jesus did not turn him away at any stage. If you are somewhere between curiosity and commitment, you are in good company.

His conversation with Jesus also gives us the heart of the Gospel. We cannot earn our way into the kingdom, even if we are as respectable as Nicodemus. We must be born again, from above, by the Spirit. And the reason Jesus came was not to condemn but because God loved the world.

The last picture of him is a good one: a man who once hid in the dark, carrying seventy-five pounds of spices through the streets of Jerusalem in broad daylight to honor Jesus. It is never too late to step forward.""", ("John", 3))])

LONG["stephen"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Stephen was", """Stephen was one of the first leaders of the church in Jerusalem, around AD 34. He was probably a Greek-speaking Jew, one of the “Hellenists,” who had been born outside Israel and spoke Greek as their first language. The Bible describes him as “a man full of faith and of the Holy Spirit.”

He was chosen as one of seven men to take care of daily food for the widows in the church. Then he became the first person to die for following Jesus.""", ("Acts", 6)),
("Why his story matters", """Stephen’s death is the first martyrdom recorded in the Bible. His story is the link between the early church in Jerusalem and the spread of the Gospel to the world. It also introduces a young man named Saul, who would become Paul.""")],
cards=[
C("The seven", "A complaint arose over food for the Greek-speaking widows. The apostles asked the church to choose seven men to handle it.",
"""The church in Jerusalem was growing fast, thousands of believers. Some Greek-speaking Jewish widows were being overlooked in the daily distribution of food. The apostles did not want to neglect prayer and the teaching of the Word to wait on tables. So they asked the church to choose seven men “of good repute, full of the Spirit and of wisdom.”

The people chose seven. Every one has a Greek name, which suggests they were chosen from the group that had complained. Stephen was first on the list. The apostles prayed and laid hands on them. It is a lovely picture of a church listening to a complaint and fixing it quickly, and of leaders giving real responsibility to others.""", ("Acts", 6)),
C("Full of grace and power", "Stephen did great wonders and signs. When people argued with him they could not match his wisdom.",
"""Stephen did more than serve tables. “And Stephen, full of grace and power, was doing great wonders and signs among the people.” He also debated with Jews from several synagogues in Jerusalem, including the synagogue of the Freedmen, people from Cyrene, Alexandria, Cilicia and Asia. Among those from Cilicia was probably a young man named Saul from Tarsus.

“But they could not withstand the wisdom and the Spirit with which he was speaking.” Unable to win the argument, they paid some men to lie about him. They said that Stephen spoke against Moses, against God, against the Temple and the law. They stirred up the people and the elders and teachers of the law, seized Stephen and brought him to the council, the Sanhedrin. “And gazing at him, all who sat in the council saw that his face was like the face of an angel.”""", ("Acts", 6)),
C("The speech", "Stephen told the history of Israel, showing how the people had often rejected the leaders God sent.",
"""The high priest asked, “Are these things so?” Stephen answered with the longest speech in the book of Acts. He started with Abraham and went through Joseph, whose brothers sold him but whom God used to save them. He talked about Moses, whom the people rejected at first when he tried to help them, though God sent him as their deliverer. He pointed out that God was not limited to the Temple: “Yet the Most High does not dwell in houses made by hands.”

Then he turned to his audience: “You stiff-necked people, uncircumcised in heart and ears, you always resist the Holy Spirit. As your fathers did, so do you. Which of the prophets did your fathers not persecute? And they killed those who announced beforehand the coming of the Righteous One, whom you have now betrayed and murdered.” His point was that the same pattern was repeating: Israel had rejected God’s messengers again, this time Jesus Himself.""", ("Acts", 7)),
C("The vision", "Stephen looked up and saw heaven open and Jesus standing at God’s right hand.",
"""“Now when they heard these things they were enraged, and they ground their teeth at him.” Stephen was completely calm. “But he, full of the Holy Spirit, gazed into heaven and saw the glory of God, and Jesus standing at the right hand of God. And he said, ‘Behold, I see the heavens opened, and the Son of Man standing at the right hand of God.’”

Elsewhere in the New Testament, Jesus is said to be seated at the right hand of God. Here, He is standing. The Bible does not explain why. Many have thought it shows Jesus rising to welcome His faithful servant, or to stand as a witness for him.

The members of the council covered their ears and shouted. They rushed at him together, dragged him out of the city and began to stone him.""", ("Acts", 7)),
C("His last words", "As he was stoned, Stephen prayed for his killers, as Jesus had done.",
"""The men who were stoning him took off their coats and laid them at the feet of a young man named Saul. As the stones struck, Stephen called out, “Lord Jesus, receive my spirit.” Then he knelt down and cried with a loud voice, “Lord, do not hold this sin against them.” “And when he had said this, he fell asleep.”

His final words echo Jesus’ words on the cross, “Father, into your hands I commit my spirit” and “Father, forgive them.” In both cases, a man facing death offered forgiveness. “Saul approved of his execution.” Right after, a great persecution broke out against the church in Jerusalem. All but the apostles were scattered through Judea and Samaria. “Now those who were scattered went about preaching the word.” Persecution intended to stop the church only spread it.""", ("Acts", 7)),
C("The seed of Paul’s story", "Saul was watching at Stephen’s death. A few years later, Jesus met Saul and turned him around.",
"""Stephen’s death was not the end of his influence. Saul, the young man who held the coats, was already leading the persecution, dragging believers out of their homes. But on the road to Damascus he met the risen Jesus, who asked him, “Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting me?” He became Paul, the apostle to the non-Jews and the author of much of the New Testament.

Many readers through the centuries have wondered whether Paul’s memory of Stephen’s face and prayer was part of what changed him. The Bible does not say exactly. But the story of Stephen is in the book of Acts right before the story of Saul’s conversion. Meanwhile, another of the seven, Philip, went to Samaria and began preaching there, and it was Philip who later met the Ethiopian official on the desert road. The scattering that began with Stephen’s death spread the message beyond Jerusalem.""", ("Acts", 9))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Stephen shows that serving others and speaking boldly can belong to the same person. He started by waiting on tables and ended by standing before the council. Faithfulness in small things prepares us for big ones.

His courage came from seeing Jesus. While the crowd was grinding their teeth, Stephen was looking at heaven. When we keep our eyes on Jesus, fear loses its grip.

And his last prayer is a model. He did not curse the men who killed him. He asked for forgiveness. It is the hardest and most Christlike thing a person can do, and it may have been the seed that changed the life of the greatest missionary in history. Forgiveness can accomplish more than revenge.""", ("Acts", 7))])

LONG["lazarus"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Lazarus was", """Lazarus lived in Bethany, a village about two miles east of Jerusalem on the road over the Mount of Olives. He had two sisters, Martha and Mary. Their home was a place Jesus often visited. The Gospel of John says, “Now Jesus loved Martha and her sister and Lazarus.” The sisters later called him “he whom you love.”

Lazarus himself never says a word in the Bible. But what happened to him is one of the greatest miracles Jesus performed, and the event that pushed the leaders to decide to kill Jesus.""", ("John", 11)),
("Why his story matters", """The raising of Lazarus is the last and greatest sign in John’s Gospel before the cross. It shows who Jesus is, what He does with grief, and how He answers death itself.""")],
cards=[
C("“He whom you love is ill”", "Lazarus fell sick. His sisters sent for Jesus, but Jesus waited two more days.",
"""When Lazarus became seriously ill, Martha and Mary sent a message to Jesus, who was across the Jordan, about a day’s journey away: “Lord, he whom you love is ill.” They did not ask Him to come. They trusted that He would know.

Jesus said, “This illness does not lead to death. It is for the glory of God, so that the Son of God may be glorified through it.” Then, surprisingly, “when he heard that Lazarus was ill, he stayed two days longer in the place where he was.” John says plainly that Jesus loved them. The delay was not because He did not care.

Then Jesus told His disciples, “Our friend Lazarus has fallen asleep, but I go to awaken him.” They thought He meant natural sleep. “Lazarus has died, and for your sake I am glad that I was not there, so that you may believe.” The disciples knew that Judea was dangerous since the leaders wanted to stone Jesus. Thomas said, “Let us also go, that we may die with him.”""", ("John", 11)),
C("Martha and Mary", "Four days after the burial, Jesus arrived. Both sisters said, “Lord, if you had been here, my brother would not have died.”",
"""When Jesus reached Bethany, Lazarus had been in the tomb four days. Many people from Jerusalem had come to comfort the sisters. Martha went out to meet Jesus. “Lord, if you had been here, my brother would not have died. But even now I know that whatever you ask from God, God will give you.”

Jesus said, “Your brother will rise again.” Martha: “I know that he will rise again in the resurrection on the last day.” Then Jesus said one of the great statements of the Gospel: “I am the resurrection and the life. Whoever believes in me, though he die, yet shall he live, and everyone who lives and believes in me shall never die. Do you believe this?” Martha answered, “Yes, Lord; I believe that you are the Christ, the Son of God, who is coming into the world.”

She went and quietly called Mary, who came and fell at Jesus’ feet. She said the same words: “Lord, if you had been here, my brother would not have died.”""", ("John", 11)),
C("Jesus wept", "Seeing Mary and the others weeping, Jesus was deeply moved and wept.",
"""“When Jesus saw her weeping, and the Jews who had come with her also weeping, he was deeply moved in his spirit and greatly troubled. And he said, ‘Where have you laid him?’ They said to him, ‘Lord, come and see.’ Jesus wept.”

It is the shortest verse in many English Bibles, and one of the most important. Jesus knew in a few minutes He would raise Lazarus. Yet He wept. He was not weeping because He had no hope, but because death is truly sorrowful, and because He loved His friends. The people said, “See how he loved him!” Some said, “Could not he who opened the eyes of the blind man also have kept this man from dying?” The Son of God does not stand at a distance from our grief. He enters it.""", ("John", 11)),
C("“Lazarus, come out”", "Jesus asked for the stone to be moved, prayed, and called Lazarus out of the tomb.",
"""The tomb was a cave with a stone across the opening. Jesus said, “Take away the stone.” Martha, practical as ever, said, “Lord, by this time there will be an odor, for he has been dead four days.” Jesus answered, “Did I not tell you that if you believed you would see the glory of God?” They took away the stone.

Jesus looked up and prayed: “Father, I thank you that you have heard me. I knew that you always hear me, but I said this on account of the people standing around, that they may believe that you sent me.” Then He shouted, “Lazarus, come out!”

“The man who had died came out, his hands and feet bound with linen strips, and his face wrapped with a cloth. Jesus said to them, ‘Unbind him, and let him go.’” Many of the Jews who had come to Mary believed in Him.""", ("John", 11)),
C("The plot", "The miracle led the chief priests to decide that Jesus must die. They also planned to kill Lazarus.",
"""Some of those who saw it reported to the Pharisees what Jesus had done. The chief priests and Pharisees called a meeting of the council. “What are we to do? For this man performs many signs. If we let him go on like this, everyone will believe in him, and the Romans will come and take away both our place and our nation.” Caiaphas, the high priest, said, “It is better for you that one man should die for the people, not that the whole nation should perish.” John comments that Caiaphas was speaking more truly than he knew. “So from that day on they made plans to put him to death.”

Six days before Passover, Jesus came back to Bethany. They gave a dinner, with Martha serving and Lazarus reclining at the table with Jesus. Mary took a pound of very expensive perfume and anointed Jesus’ feet, wiping them with her hair. Large crowds came to see Jesus and also Lazarus. “So the chief priests made plans to put Lazarus to death as well, because on account of him many of the Jews were going over and believing in Jesus.”""", ("John", 12))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Lazarus’ story is for anyone who has prayed and not gotten the answer they wanted on the timetable they wanted. Jesus stayed two days longer, and Martha and Mary were hurt. But He was not late. He was working toward something greater than healing: raising a man who had been dead four days.

It also tells us what Jesus does with grief. He does not scold. He weeps. If you are grieving, the Bible says Jesus knows what that is.

And it points to the greatest promise. Jesus said, “I am the resurrection and the life.” Lazarus’ rising was a sign of what Jesus would do Himself a few weeks later and what He will do for all who believe in Him. Death is not the last word.""", ("John", 11))])

LONG["joseph-nazareth"] = dict(intro=[
("Who Joseph was", """Joseph was a craftsman, or tekton in Greek, a word that can mean carpenter, builder or stonemason. He lived in Nazareth, a small village in Galilee. He was a descendant of King David through a family line that had once ruled in Jerusalem but was now living in modest circumstances. Matthew’s Gospel calls him “a just man,” someone who cared about doing right.

He was engaged to Mary, a young woman of Nazareth. A Jewish engagement was a legal commitment, usually lasting a year, before the husband took his wife home.""", ("Matthew", 1)),
("Why his story matters", """Joseph never speaks a word in the Bible. But he was chosen to be the earthly father of the Son of God, and he protected Jesus and Mary through scandal, danger and exile. His quiet obedience is a model of faith.""")],
cards=[
C("A hard discovery", "Joseph learned that Mary was pregnant and decided to end the engagement quietly.",
"""Joseph found out that Mary was expecting a child, and he knew he was not the father. In that world, it was a grave matter. The law allowed him to publicly shame her, and in some cases, much worse. But the Bible says, “Joseph, her husband, being a just man and unwilling to put her to shame, resolved to divorce her quietly.”

Think about the pain. He had to believe that the woman he loved had been unfaithful, or else believe an astonishing story. He chose mercy. Before he could act, he went to sleep, and an angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream: “Joseph, son of David, do not fear to take Mary as your wife, for that which is conceived in her is from the Holy Spirit. She will bear a son, and you shall call his name Jesus, for he will save his people from their sins.” The name Jesus means “the Lord saves.”""", ("Matthew", 1)),
C("Obedience and Bethlehem", "Joseph took Mary as his wife and traveled to Bethlehem for the census, where Jesus was born.",
"""“When Joseph woke from sleep, he did as the angel of the Lord commanded him: he took his wife.” He chose to accept the scandal and the gossip of a small town. That meant standing next to Mary in public.

Months later, the Roman emperor Augustus ordered a census. Everyone had to register in their ancestral town. For Joseph, a descendant of David, that meant Bethlehem, about 90 miles south of Nazareth. Mary, who was about to give birth, went with him. The town was crowded, and there was no place for them in the guest room. Jesus was born in Bethlehem, in humble surroundings, and laid in a manger, a feeding trough. Joseph gave Him the name Jesus on the eighth day, as the angel had said. Shepherds came with news of angels, and Joseph listened to their story with Mary.""", ("Luke", 2)),
C("At the Temple", "Forty days after the birth, Joseph and Mary brought Jesus to the Temple. They offered two small birds, the offering of the poor.",
"""According to the law of Moses, a mother was to present an offering after the birth of a child. A lamb was the normal offering, but the poor could offer two doves or pigeons. Joseph and Mary brought two birds, a sign that they did not have much.

At the Temple, an old man named Simeon, who had been promised he would see the Messiah, took Jesus in his arms and thanked God. “My eyes have seen your salvation.” He told Mary that a sword would pierce her own soul. An elderly prophetess named Anna also thanked God and told everyone who was waiting for the redemption of Jerusalem. “And his father and his mother marveled at what was said about him.” Joseph was amazed, too. He was raising a child who was going to change the world.""", ("Luke", 2)),
C("Flight to Egypt", "An angel warned Joseph in a dream to take the family to Egypt to escape Herod. He left that night.",
"""Wise men from the East came to Jerusalem looking for the king of the Jews, and Herod heard of it. Herod decided to kill the child. “An angel of the Lord appeared to Joseph in a dream and said, ‘Rise, take the child and his mother, and flee to Egypt, and remain there until I tell you, for Herod is about to search for the child, to destroy him.’ And he rose and took the child and his mother by night and departed to Egypt.”

Notice that Joseph did not argue or ask for time. He got up in the middle of the night. They left behind home, work and family to live as refugees in a foreign country. The wise men’s gold may have helped provide for the family. They stayed until Herod died.

Then an angel appeared again: “Rise, take the child and his mother and go to the land of Israel.” Learning that Herod’s son Archelaus was ruling Judea, Joseph was afraid, and a dream directed him to the region of Galilee. “And he went and lived in a city called Nazareth.”""", ("Matthew", 2)),
C("A father at home", "Joseph raised Jesus in Nazareth, taught Him his trade, and took the family to Jerusalem each Passover.",
"""In Nazareth, Joseph worked as a craftsman, and Jesus learned the trade. Later, people in Nazareth said of Jesus, “Is not this the carpenter’s son?” Joseph also saw to Jesus’ religious upbringing. “Now his parents went to Jerusalem every year at the Feast of the Passover.” Luke says that Jesus “grew and became strong, filled with wisdom. And the favor of God was upon him.”

When Jesus was twelve, the family went to Jerusalem for Passover. On the way back, they could not find Him. After three days of searching, they found Him in the Temple, sitting among the teachers, asking and listening. Mary said, “Son, why have you treated us so? Behold, your father and I have been searching for you in great distress.” “Why were you looking for me? Did you not know that I must be in my Father’s house?” Even so, “he went down with them and came to Nazareth and was submissive to them.”

This is the last scene in the Bible where Joseph appears. He is not mentioned during Jesus’ ministry. The Bible does not say exactly what happened to him, but because Jesus entrusted His mother to John at the cross, it seems likely that Joseph had died by then.""", ("Luke", 2))],
outro=[
("What does this mean for us today?", """Joseph never says a word in the Bible, but his actions say a great deal. He chose mercy when he could have chosen shame. He obeyed immediately each time God spoke. He protected a child who was not biologically his. He taught Jesus a trade and took Him to the Temple.

His story reminds us that God often works through quiet, ordinary faithfulness. There were no crowds cheering for Joseph. Most of what he did was daily work, being a husband and father, and doing it faithfully.

And he shows what fatherhood can look like. The Bible says that Jesus was “submissive” to Joseph and Mary and grew in wisdom. A father or any caregiver who is present, protects and teaches shapes a life. Joseph’s name does not appear in a long speech, but the world has not forgotten him.""", ("Matthew", 1))])
